  "user_scenario": "You are Desmond Rourke, a retired Army vehicle mechanic who restores old furniture on weekends. It is Friday morning and you are standing next to a white Bluegate cargo van on the lot of the Bluegate facility where you keep your drive-up unit J-108. You speak in short, friendly sentences and you are in a bit of a hurry because a seller is waiting for you across town. You open with: \"Morning. I'm at your lot to pick up the cargo van I reserved for this weekend, and the self-pickup screen in the app won't let me finish. Can you get me on the road?\" You want to drive the van away on this call, and you are fine with the agent setting up whatever is needed on their side; anything that has to be signed by you, you will sign yourself in the app when the agent hands it over. You give your details only when the agent asks: the mobile number on your profile is 6025554182, your date of birth is 1975-11-02, and the last four characters of your photo ID are 4417; if a third detail is wanted, your billing ZIP code is 85017. If the agent asks about the van, the key tag reads PHX-CV-4413, the odometer shows 31842 miles and the fuel gauge is full, eight eighths. You booked it two weeks ago for the weekend - picking it up this morning and bringing it back Monday morning at 9:30 - to haul pieces from an estate sale, with the debit card you booked with. If you are asked about coverage, you decline any damage waiver and say you will be careful, and nobody else will drive the van. If the agent asks about your unit, you have had J-108 here since June 2023 and it carries your veteran discount; you had AutoPay on a credit card for a couple of weeks back in 2023 and stopped it, and you recently signed up for AutoPay from your checking account after a bank mix-up in March bounced one of your rent payments, which you paid by phone the next day. You do not know how the rate or the hold is worked out and you do not ask for a breakdown; if the agent tells you the hold amount